WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:09.050
We've got blood tests,
we've got nasal swabs,

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:12.360
all kinds of ways to
detect the coronavirus,

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:15.953
and now we can add to that mix, poop,

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:19.623
specifically, wastewater.

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:22.760
There's a company called Biobot

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:24.780
that's analyzing this wastewater

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:27.280
to try and look for the coronavirus.

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:29.960
And they've found that in a lot of cases,

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:32.320
the virus is much more widespread

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:35.450
than the more conventional
test would show.

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:38.250
So let's head off to Massachusetts

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.803
to interview the founders of Biobot.

00:00:52.663 --> 00:00:54.160
Would you two mind introducing yourselves,

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:55.820
maybe Mariana first?

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:57.400
My name is Mariana Matus.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:01.100
I am co-founder and CEO
of Biobot Analytics.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:02.810
My name is Newsha Ghaeli,

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:06.560
and I am co-founder and
president at Biobot Analytics.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:07.940
Your company, the two of you have come up

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:12.190
with a pretty unique way
to look at the coronavirus.

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:14.440
I mean, you're talking about sewage

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:17.780
coming from different parts
of the country, right?

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:18.680
Correct.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:21.860
We can detect the virus in wastewater

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:26.710
because people who are infected
shed the virus in stool,

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:30.640
and it makes its way into the
wastewater infrastructure.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.960
Then wastewater treatment
plants collect samples

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:35.810
and send them to us.

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:38.640
Right now, Biobot has a pro bono campaign,

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:42.630
and we are testing about
10% of the US population

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:47.110
on a weekly basis, versus
about 1% of the US population

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:49.280
that is being tested through the clinics.

00:01:50.213 --> 00:01:52.450
And the results that you've already seen

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:53.480
are pretty striking.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:54.613
Yeah.

00:01:54.613 --> 00:01:56.490
Our first results were gathered

00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:59.736
from a large metropolitan
region in Massachusetts.

00:01:59.736 --> 00:02:02.820
And on the day that the
samples were collected,

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:07.030
there were about 450
confirmed clinical cases

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:08.540
in that community.

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:12.120
And what we were able to
see via sewage testing

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:15.820
is that there were up to 100,000 people

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:20.200
infected with coronavirus
in that catchment area.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:21.500
So just dramatically more

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:23.540
than what the regular tests were showing?

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:24.840
Yeah, exactly.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:28.780
There's actually a large
asymptomatic population, likely,

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:31.700
or individuals who have
very mild symptoms.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:34.070
and so they're not seeking out testing.

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:37.470
However, they're captured and
represented in sewage data,

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:40.140
and so we're seeing them in our tests.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:43.454
And I know you get a 50-milliliter sample.

00:02:43.454 --> 00:02:47.963
How is that representative of
an entire city's population?

00:02:49.270 --> 00:02:51.240
So, treatment plants,

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:53.270
they can sample the wastewater

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:56.043
as it comes into the
treatment plant constantly

00:02:56.043 --> 00:03:00.550
through 24 hours to guarantee
that we get representation

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:04.430
from every toilet flush that
went through that community.

00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:06.720
And then from that aggregate sample,

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:10.186
they are taking a small
aliquot and sending that to us.

00:03:10.186 --> 00:03:12.220
And using that sample,

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:15.746
we measure how much virus
was present in the wastewater

00:03:15.746 --> 00:03:18.400
with a technique called QPCR,

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:21.770
that looks for the genetic
signature of the virus.

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:22.603
Okay.

00:03:22.603 --> 00:03:24.430
And you'd already had this
company up and running

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:26.530
to look for other, what,

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:29.080
other diseases in wastewater already?

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:29.980
Correct.

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:34.240
We originally were working
with the opioid epidemic,

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:36.530
measuring the concentration
of different types

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:39.149
of prescription opioids in wastewater

00:03:39.149 --> 00:03:41.550
to create better programs.

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:43.450
It's not the most glamorous job, maybe.

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:46.780
What drew you to this field?

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:49.290
I really grew up with hardship,

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:53.160
so I've always been passionate
about creating technologies

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:56.990
that help with equity and
just bringing better resources

00:03:56.990 --> 00:03:58.720
to people who need it.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:03.210
And the beauty of looking at
wastewater as a diagnostic

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:06.680
is that everybody has
a voice in the sewer.

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:10.078
You don't need to have the
money to go to the doctor.

00:04:10.078 --> 00:04:11.970
You don't need to have medical insurance.

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:14.760
As long as your house is
connected to the wastewater,

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:16.603
your information is counted,

00:04:16.603 --> 00:04:21.601
and we can bring that to
create better programming.

00:04:21.601 --> 00:04:23.130
What's your vision for how this plays out

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:25.120
over the coming years?

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:28.780
Yeah, our vision is that
one day this technology

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:33.730
will be basic infrastructure
for every city

00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:36.230
and town worldwide.

00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:40.200
So we will be constantly
screening our populations

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:43.280
for new outbreaks of
the novel coronavirus.

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:46.580
And in this next week,
two weeks, three weeks,

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:47.900
there's just going to
be more and more data

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:50.370
coming out, obviously,
from the work you're doing.

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:52.420
All of the facilities
that we're working with

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:56.491
really believe that weekly
testing is very important.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:01.350
One of the biggest
questions facing government

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:04.810
and decision makers
right now is how and when

00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:07.220
can we start to safely
open up our cities again

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:08.320
and our economies again,

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:13.110
and testing at scale is one
of the most critical things

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:14.950
in order to help us get there.

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:17.050
And we're not going to be able to test

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:20.360
every single individual;
that's just not realistic.

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:24.070
And so, this type of testing
can really be helpful

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:25.830
in making these types of decisions

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:28.500
of scaling back quarantine-style measures

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:30.170
or social distancing.

00:05:30.170 --> 00:05:33.120
And so, all of the facilities
that we're working with

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:35.050
are sending us weekly samples,

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:37.450
we're giving weekly data back to them,

00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:41.563
and, yeah, we're excited
to see how that progresses.

